const PAGE_HTML = "<h1>Terms and Conditions Draft Notes</h1>\n<p>This is a working product/legal draft for review by counsel before any public launch. It is intentionally plain-language so the product can be designed around the risk model.</p>\n<h2>Media ownership</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Customers retain ownership of all uploaded, imported, or generated media.</li>\n<li>ShootFlow should only request the permissions needed to copy, organize, analyze, grade, and export deliverables.</li>\n<li>Any model-training use of customer media should be opt-in, explicit, and separately revocable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>File handling and deletion</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The product is designed to copy source media, not delete it.</li>\n<li>Source deletion, card formatting, destructive overwrite, and archive cleanup are excluded from early product scope.</li>\n<li>Generated deliverables should be written through safe output paths and should not overwrite user/editor-created files unless the product can prove the existing bytes match the intended output or the user explicitly approves replacement.</li>\n<li>If destructive actions are ever added, they must require explicit confirmation, visible manifest review, and a recoverable holding period where technically possible.</li>\n<li>Drive detection and import suggestions should be treated as recommendations until the user reviews the source and destination. The legal terms should avoid promising that the product can identify every SD card, SSD, corrupt file, or unsafe path perfectly.</li>\n<li>Mounted-volume discovery should be framed as advisory scanning of user/system-provided locations. Skipping symlinks, files, inaccessible paths, or unusual devices is a safety feature, not a guarantee that every connected device will be detected.</li>\n<li>Native volume metadata such as display names, removable status, or read-only\nflags should also be framed as advisory. It can improve recommendations, but\nit does not guarantee correct device classification and does not override\nfilesystem checks, user review, checksum verification, or backup duties.</li>\n<li>Generated manifests, render status files, and scheduler reports should be presented as operational records, not as a substitute for independent backups or professional media archive practices.</li>\n<li>Workspace index files should be presented as metadata summaries of operational\nrecords. They do not expand renderer authority, prove backup completeness, or\nmake standalone AI/storyline artifacts safe to execute without the underlying\nmanifest-backed validation.</li>\n<li>Command-line ingest automation requires an explicit <code>--confirm</code> flag before any media is copied. Discovery and preview commands are advisory; they never copy files, never write a manifest, and never delete media. Even with <code>--confirm</code>, source media is not deleted, destination files are never overwritten with different bytes, and every copy is verified by SHA-256 before the manifest is written.</li>\n<li>The unified card-dump workflow follows the same boundary: without\n<code>--confirm-ingest</code> it only discovers routes, estimates source media, and\npreviews checksums; with <code>--confirm-ingest</code> it performs the verified copy and\nthen runs read-only production planning. It does not add source deletion,\nformatting, cleanup, or overwrite authority.</li>\n<li>Post-ingest production planning is read-only. The current <code>plan-production</code>\ncommand reloads and validates the manifest, summarizes planned work, and does\nnot create analysis, graded, render-status, or deliverable files.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Customer responsibilities</h2>\n<p>Customers are responsible for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Maintaining independent backups of original media.</li>\n<li>Confirming they have rights to process and publish people, music, trademarks, locations, and client materials in their shoots.</li>\n<li>Reviewing AI-generated edits, captions, selections, and exports before publishing.</li>\n<li>Complying with platform rules for Instagram, TikTok, YouTube, and other distribution channels.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>AI output disclaimer</h2>\n<p>ShootFlow may use computer vision, VLMs, LLMs, and automated editing models to suggest selects, grades, reel variants, captions, and publishing packages. Outputs may be inaccurate, incomplete, off-brand, duplicative, or unsuitable for a specific client. Customers must review outputs before client delivery or publication.</p>\n<h2>Indemnification concept</h2>\n<p>Customers should indemnify and hold harmless the company, its affiliates, employees, contractors, and service providers from claims arising out of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Customer media, including rights of publicity, privacy, copyright, trademark, music, venue restrictions, and client contractual restrictions.</li>\n<li>Customer publication, client delivery, paid advertising, or distribution of generated content.</li>\n<li>Customer misuse of the service, including attempts to process unlawful, infringing, or non-consensual content.</li>\n<li>Customer failure to maintain backups before ingest or editing workflows.</li>\n<li>Customer instructions, prompts, presets, reference images, brand assets, music, captions, and publishing metadata supplied to the service.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The company should indemnify customers only for narrow claims that the hosted software, as provided by the company and used according to the terms, infringes third-party intellectual property rights, subject to standard exclusions for customer media, modifications, unsupported workflows, and third-party services.</p>\n<h2>Draft clause language (for counsel refinement)</h2>\n<p>The following is intentionally non-final and should be reviewed by counsel\nbefore launch.</p>\n<h3>Customer indemnification (draft)</h3>\n<p>&quot;You will defend, indemnify, and hold harmless Company, its affiliates, and\ntheir respective officers, directors, employees, contractors, and agents from\nand against any third-party claims, liabilities, damages, losses, and expenses\n(including reasonable attorneys&#39; fees) arising out of or related to: (a) Your\nMedia; (b) Your use of the Services in violation of applicable law, platform\nterms, or third-party rights; (c) Your instructions, prompts, presets, metadata,\nor publication decisions; or (d) Your failure to maintain independent backups of\nsource media.&quot;</p>\n<h3>Company indemnification (draft)</h3>\n<p>&quot;Company will defend You against third-party claims alleging that the Services,\nas provided by Company and used by You in accordance with this Agreement,\ninfringe such third party&#39;s intellectual property rights, and will indemnify You\nfor resulting damages and settlement amounts, subject to exclusions for\ncustomer-provided media/content, customer modifications, combinations not\nprovided by Company, and use of unsupported third-party systems.&quot;</p>\n<h3>File safety and deletion boundary (draft)</h3>\n<p>&quot;Unless expressly enabled in a separately documented feature with explicit user\nconfirmation, the Services are designed to copy and organize media, not delete\nsource media. You acknowledge that device detection, route recommendations, and\nautomation suggestions are advisory, may be incomplete, and do not replace Your\nindependent verification and backup practices.&quot;</p>\n<h3>Limitation of liability (draft)</h3>\n<p>&quot;To the maximum extent permitted by law, neither party will be liable for any\nindirect, incidental, special, consequential, exemplary, or punitive damages,\nor for any loss of profits, revenue, goodwill, use, data, media, or business\ninterruption, arising out of or related to the Services, even if advised of the\npossibility of those damages. Company&#39;s aggregate liability for all claims\narising out of or relating to the Services will not exceed the fees paid by You\nfor the Services during the twelve (12) months before the event giving rise to\nthe claim (or, for free-tier users, a nominal cap to be defined in final terms).&quot;</p>\n<h3>Customer backup and verification acknowledgment (draft)</h3>\n<p>&quot;You acknowledge that You are solely responsible for maintaining independent\ncopies of source media and verifying successful import, backup, and export\nresults before deleting, formatting, or repurposing source devices. Operational\nrecords produced by the Services (including manifests and status reports) are\nprovided as workflow aids and do not constitute a backup guarantee.&quot;</p>\n<h2>Limitation of liability concept</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Exclude liability for lost profits, lost business, reputational harm, lost data, corrupted media, platform account penalties, and indirect damages to the maximum extent allowed by law.</li>\n<li>Cap direct damages at fees paid during a recent lookback period or a fixed amount for free users.</li>\n<li>Avoid representing that automated edits will increase revenue, engagement, quality, or client satisfaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Privacy and safety principles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Encrypt customer media in transit and at rest.</li>\n<li>Separate customer workspaces and access controls.</li>\n<li>Keep audit logs for ingest, export, and destructive-intent actions.</li>\n<li>Provide deletion/export rights for account data while preserving legal and abuse-prevention records where required.</li>\n<li>Make subprocessors and AI providers visible before launch.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Product copy constraints</h2>\n<p>Marketing should say the product saves time, increases throughput, and produces reviewable drafts. It should not promise guaranteed viral content, guaranteed client acceptance, or perfect color fidelity without human review.</p>\n";

